  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Through Friday Fog & Low Cloud Potential: Strong high pressure\n   increasing likelihood of valley inversions.\n\n-  Pattern Shift: A Pacific system will tap into atmospheric river\n   moisture beginning Saturday, snow levels stay above 6000 feet \n   and precipitation amounts light.\n\n-  Further cooling early to mid next week with potential for snow\n   levels to lower further.\n\nHigh pressure was sufficient enough to produce morning valley fog\nbut low level mixing has broken inversions and dissipated fog.\nHigh clouds are moving over the area yet temperatures will warm \nwell above normal for this time of year in most locations. \nClearing overnight may allow for some radiational cooling and \nreintroduction of valley fog Thursday morning.\n\nPacific moisture wraps around a powerful Pacific low pressure\nsystem as it drops south along the west coast Friday. While it \nwill bring a warm southerly flow, it will also increase clouds \nwhich in turn will limit warming Friday and Saturday (even limited\nwarming will run 10 degrees above normal in locations). A little \nlight precipitation is expected in north- central and Lemhi County\nID Saturday with no impacts expected. \n\nThe next push of moisture arrives late Monday affecting the region\nthrough Wednesday morning. Precipitation amounts are light but\nhave trended up over the past couple model runs. Additionally,\nsnow levels that will remain above 6000 feet this weekend slowly\nlower to around 4000 feet by Wednesday morning. A light\naccumulation may develop by Wednesday morning in higher valleys of\nsouthwest MT and Lemhi County ID, but in the Anaconda and Pintler\nMountains a few to 6 inches of snow is possible along with \nperiods of slick driving conditions over MacDonald and Lost Trail \nPasses. While impacts should be limited to near the passes, they \nwill also dissipate rapidly as the sun rises Wednesday. If \nprecipitation amounts continue to trend up, impacts could increase\nand expand, stay updated to future forecasts.",
